== God Takes Notice of the Least of His Saints

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The words, explained in this way, give us two special points of doctrine.]

1\. #emph[Doctrine.] God takes notice of every one of his saints, even of the least of them, and of kindness shown to them.

#emph[Joseph] was only a lad, and was sold as a slave. Yet because he was one of the church, one of the little ones, yes, #emph[minimorum minimus] \[the least of the least\], God takes notice of him in #emph[Egypt,] and of the kindness that was shown to him (Gen 39:21). Many other such examples are noted in the Scripture, but none fits our purpose better than the example of #emph[Lazarus] (Luke 16:20). Though he was a very poor beggar, full of sores, and despised by #emph[Dives] [the rich man of Luke 16] and all his household, yet God took such notice of him that he made his angels attend him. Yes, God took notice of the kindness which the dogs showed him, for their licking of his sores is recorded for all ages. Note these expressions of the #emph[psalmist:] “But I am poor and needy; yet the Lord thinks of me” (Ps 40:17). And again: “This poor man called out, and the #smallcaps[Lord] heard him; He saved him from all his troubles” (Ps 34:6).

Question. If you#super[+] ask why the Lord takes notice of every one of his saints:

Answer 1. #emph[They are all his children, and of his household.] So, as a tender and careful father and master, he cares for every one. Yes, they are all members of the body of Christ. Now the head takes care of every member, even the least, and takes notice of every kindness done to any of them.

2\. #emph[Because of the grace in them, they are all like jewels] and #emph[precious stones.] So, as a provident #emph[jeweler,] he will look after every one of them. Yes, what often appears least may be of greater worth, as a little #emph[diamond] is of more value than a far greater pebble.

This gives a singular ground of comfort to those who are desolate and forsaken in this world. They may be left alone (as #emph[Elijah was,] 1 Kgs 19:10), “like a desert owl, like an owl among the ruins,” “like a lone bird on a housetop,” as the psalmist expresses it (Ps 102:6, 7). Yet the Lord is with them and takes care of them. Now if the Lord is with us and takes care of us, what need we fear? How this should encourage us to extend our charity to any one of the saints and members of Christ, though they are lowly and little in the world and not able to repay us! And though no one takes notice of it, let us consider that the Lord takes notice, and will abundantly reward every good work, yes, the least kindness we do to them. Heb 6:10: “God is not unjust. He will not forget your#super[+] work and labor of love which you#super[+] have shown for His name, as you#super[+] have ministered to the saints and continue to do so.” Here #emph[God’s not forgetting their labor of love in ministering to the saints] implies that, as God takes notice of their kindness, so he is, and always will be, mindful of such persons, to support and help them and do them good in every way.

And if God will remember in this way those who remember the least of his, how will he take it if they are neglected? Beloved, how is it with God’s little ones at this day? Is it not very low with many of them? God takes notice how very low it is. And whether you#super[+] will or not, God will not forsake them. But (to use the words of #emph[Mordecai] to #emph[Esther] with some variation, Esth 4:14) if you#super[+] altogether hold back your#super[+] hand at this time, relief will arise for them some other way, but you#super[+] and your#super[+] house (take heed that this word is not fulfilled on you#super[+]) will be destroyed. But who knows whether you#super[+] have come to your#super[+] estates for such a time as this!
